            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for getting back with the demo!\r\n\r\nReading a bit more about how `vim.paste()` (and pasting from system register in general) works, this indeed might be in issue. But only if scroll animation is enabled **and** terminal emulator implements streamed paste. That is, it pastes lines at cursor position in chunks which might trigger scroll animation (if they are big enough to move window view). And as scroll animation also changes positioning of cursor, this might lead to \"scrambled\" text.\r\n\r\nThe reason `\"+p` seems to work is because it pastes whole text at once *and* does not move cursor to the end of the pasted text.\r\n\r\nAs I don't have access to iTerm2, could you try the following wrapper for `vim.paste` and get back with whether it has fixed the issue?\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nvim.paste = function(_, phase)\r\n  if phase == -1 or phase == 3 then vim.fn.feedkeys('\"+p', 'nx') end\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nI've tested that this works in `st` and `xfce4-terminal` (both only implement non-streaming paste), `Kitty` and `Ghostty` (both implement proper streamed paste). It does not work with `WezTerm` (which does not implement proper streamed paste), so using plain `\"+p` is recommended here.",
